<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl Linnaeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1707">1707</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Carl von Linné, Linnaeus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl Linnaeus was a Swedish botanist, physician and zoologist, professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala-university">Uppsala University</a>, who formalised the modern system of naming and classifying living things. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/systema-naturae">Systema Naturae</a> of 1735 set out a hierarchical classification of nature, and his Species Plantarum of 1753 established the two-part <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> names, genus and species, still used for plants and animals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus.jpg" alt="Carl Linnaeus" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Roslin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_von_Linn%C3%A9,_1707-1778,_botanist,_professor_(Alexander_Roslin)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_15723_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carl Linnaeus was born on 23 May 1707 in the village of Råshult in Småland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, the first child of Nicolaus (Nils) Linnaeus and Christina Brodersonia. His father, a Lutheran minister and amateur botanist, taught him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as a small child, and the family moved to the rectory of Stenbrohult when his father became rector there. Linnaeus liked plants from his earliest years, and his father gave him his own patch of land to grow them.</p><p>In 1727, at the age of 21, he enrolled at Lund University. Most of his higher education was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala-university">Uppsala University</a>, where he began giving lectures in botany in 1730. In May 1732 he set out on an expedition to Lapland, funded by a grant from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> of Sciences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>, curious about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a> people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In April 1735 Linnaeus travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> to study medicine at the University of Harderwijk, and he published the first edition of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/systema-naturae">Systema Naturae</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. After returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> on 28 June 1738 he worked as a physician at the Admiralty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> and helped found the Royal Swedish Academy of Science, becoming its first Praeses by drawing of lots. He later became professor of medicine and botany at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>, and in the 1740s he was sent on several journeys through Sweden to find and classify plants and animals.</p><p>He taught many students, 17 of whom he called his apostles, and they made botanical expeditions around the world, so that his system spread without his travelling outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> again. He was ennobled in 1761. Illness troubled his last years, and he died on 10 January 1778 in Hammarby.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The first edition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/systema-naturae">Systema Naturae</a>, printed in 1735, ran to twelve pages. By its 10th edition in 1758 it classified 4,400 species of animals and 7,700 species of plants. Linnaeus's main contribution to taxonomy was the establishment of universally accepted conventions for naming organisms, and his work marks the starting point of the consistent use of binomial nomenclature.</p><p>The Linnaean system placed nature in a nested hierarchy of three kingdoms divided into classes, orders, genera and species. His taxonomy of human races has been criticised in the 21st century: some regard him as a forebear of scientific racism, while others hold that his classification was stereotyped but did not imply that any race was superior.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carl Linnaeus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Swedish scientists included <b>Carl Linnaeus</b>, whose Systema Naturae (1735) founded modern biological classification, and Anders Celsius, and the will of Alfred Nobel created the Nobel Prizes, first awarded in 1901.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Latin for two more centuries: Copernicus’s On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres of 1543, William Harvey’s account of the circulation of the blood, Newton’s Principia of 1687 and the works in which <b>Carl Linnaeus</b> named plants and animals with two Latin words were all written in it.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Råshult, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in Linnaeus Hammarby, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.62,14.2&amp;year=1753" aria-label="Råshult on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4857 240 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carl Linnaeus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5307 510h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5307 510h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.62,14.2&amp;year=1753">What was here in 1753? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If the summer be indeed shorter here than in any other part of the world, it must be allowed, at the same time, to be no where more delightful. I was never in my life in better health than at present.&#8221;</p><footer>Lachesis Lapponica, the journal of his tour in Lapland (1732) &#183; tr. Charles Troilius, 1811 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34779/pg34779-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I witnessed with pleasure the supreme tranquillity enjoyed by the inhabitants of this sequestered country.&#8221;</p><footer>Lachesis Lapponica, the journal of his tour in Lapland (1732) &#183; tr. Charles Troilius, 1811 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34779/pg34779-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have a notion that Adam and Eve were giants, and that mankind from one generation to another, owing to poverty and other causes, have diminished in size.&#8221;</p><footer>Lachesis Lapponica, the journal of his tour in Lapland (1732) &#183; tr. Charles Troilius, 1811 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34779/pg34779-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
